# Reflection — looking at code as data

Inspect and manipulate classes, methods, and fields **at runtime** — code as data you can query and act on. The engine under most frameworks.

## The `Class` object

```java
Class<?> c  = user.getClass();                 // from an instance
Class<?> c2 = User.class;                       // from the type
Class<?> c3 = Class.forName("com.acme.User");   // from a string!

for (Method m : c.getDeclaredMethods())
    System.out.println(m.getName());
```

## Acting, not just reading

```java
Method greet = c.getMethod("greet", String.class);
greet.invoke(user, "Ana");        // call by name at runtime
ctor.newInstance();               // construct reflectively
field.setAccessible(true);        // even private members
```

## Why — it powers your frameworks

- **DI** (Spring) · **JSON** (Jackson) · **JUnit** finds `@Test` · **ORMs** (Hibernate)

Each operates on *your* classes it never saw at compile time.

## Costs — use sparingly

**Slower** · **no compile-time safety** (rename → runtime break) · **breaks encapsulation** (modules can forbid). Prefer ordinary calls.
